<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chincha Islands War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands">Chincha Islands</a> War was a conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its former colonies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, later joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, from 1864 to 1866. It began when a Spanish squadron seized Peru's guano-rich Chincha Islands. In 1866 the Spanish bombarded the undefended port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> and then attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> before withdrawing; a final peace was signed only in the 1870s and 1880s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chincha-islands-war.jpg" alt="Chincha Islands War" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CombateDosdeMayo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under Isabella II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> greatly increased its military spending and became one of the larger naval powers, active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. At the end of 1862 it sent a scientific expedition to South American waters under Admiral Luis Hernández-Pinzón Álvarez, with the covert aim of backing the claims of Spanish citizens there. On 4 August 1863 a fight at the Talambo hacienda in Lambayeque, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, left one Spaniard dead and four injured. Pinzón demanded an apology and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, which Peru refused as an internal police matter, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> added a demand for debts from the War of Independence.</p><p>Its envoy, Eusebio Salazar y Mazarredo, arrived in March 1864 with the title of Royal Commissary, a colonial office rather than an ambassador's, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> took as an insult. On 14 April 1864 the Spanish seized the islands, which produced almost 60% of the Peruvian government's annual revenue, arrested their governor and landed 400 marines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Vice Admiral José Manuel Pareja, sent to replace Pinzón, signed the Vivanco-Pareja Treaty on 27 January 1865, but Peruvian opinion saw it as a blow to national honour, and President Juan Antonio Pezet fell on 7 November. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> to Spanish ships, Pareja demanded a 21-gun salute at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> on 17 September 1865; Chile refused and war was declared on 24 September. After the Chilean corvette Esmeralda captured the Spanish schooner Covadonga at the Battle of Papudo on 26 November, Pareja took his own life, and Casto Méndez Núñez took command.</p><p>Under General Mariano Ignacio Prado, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> on 5 December 1865 and declared war on 14 January 1866; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> followed on 30 January and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> on 22 March, closing every Pacific port south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> to the Spanish. An exchange of fire at Abtao on 7 February ended indecisively. On 31 March the Spanish shelled and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a>, sinking or burning 33 vessels, and on 2 May they attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a>, where both sides claimed victory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Without ports for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and supplies, the Spanish fleet left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chincha-islands">Chincha Islands</a> and returned home by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, completing a circumnavigation. Whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> planned to recover its former colonies is unknown. Prado presented the war as a national victory and ordered the ironclads Independencia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huascar">Huáscar</a>, the first armoured ships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, while in Chile Federico Errázuriz Zañartu, secretary of war during the conflict, later worked to modernise the army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chincha Islands War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
